<p>A database creates maintenance work while it serves requests. Updating an order can leave an earlier row version behind. A bulk import changes the distribution the query planner relies on. A disconnected replica can keep transaction logs on the writer. The data may be correct while this unfinished work steadily consumes the room the next change needs.</p>
<p>In the shop from <a href="/review/relational-next/operations/">Running a relational service</a>, fulfilment marks orders paid, staff run reports and an operator maintains PostgreSQL. We will first make cleanup progress, then change the engine version without confusing “the command finished” with “the service is ready”. The same pattern governs both: identify the required result, establish what can prevent it, fit the work within a budget and test the result before removing the return path.</p>
<p>PostgreSQL 18 supplies the concrete operating contract. A disposable native rehearsal ran maintenance on 18.6 and a real copy-mode upgrade from 17.6 to 18.6. These are the installed versions used for evidence; 17.6 is not a recommendation for a current source installation. The figures explain mechanisms and decisions, and never execute database commands.</p>

<nav aria-label="In this article" class="article-contents"><ol>{sections.map(([id,title])=><li><a href={`#${id}`}>{title}</a></li>)}</ol></nav>

<h2 id="identify-the-work-the-service-keeps-creating">Identify the work the service keeps creating</h2>
<p>Begin with the workload and the engine. PostgreSQL keeps obsolete row versions in table pages until they can be removed. Vacuum makes their space reusable, maintains visibility information and freezes sufficiently old transaction identifiers. Analyse collects the statistics used to choose plans. Archiving and replication consume transaction history. These duties have different completion tests; a successful vacuum does not refresh every statistic or repair a failed archive.</p>
<p><strong>Autovacuum</strong> schedules vacuum and analyse work. It needs enough opportunity and resources to finish. The operator still owns evidence of progress and the response when it falls behind. Use the parent’s <a href="/review/relational-next/operations/#assign-every-operating-responsibility">responsibility map</a> to identify the provider’s duties and yours. For a managed service, establish which upkeep controls, logs and escalation paths it exposes.</p>
<p>The shop’s daily inventory can be short: changing tables and cleanup age; statistics after imports; retained history and its consumers; storage headroom; engine, extension and operating-system support dates. Put an owner and last accepted result beside each. Schedule because that result needs maintaining, rather than running the same expensive command on every table each Sunday.</p>

<h2 id="diagnose-upkeep-before-choosing-a-command">Diagnose upkeep before choosing a command</h2>
<p>A backlog is work not yet completed. An impediment is something that prevents completion. A file that remains large after cleanup can be neither: it may contain reusable space. Distinguish these before changing the service.</p>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Maintenance evidence and the next distinction"><table><thead><tr><th>Observation</th><th>Establish next</th><th>Possible action and completion test</th></tr></thead><tbody>
 <tr><td>Dead-version estimate and table size rise</td><td>Needed history, insufficient cleanup opportunity, or both?</td><td>Resolve the history owner or adjust upkeep; verify removability and continued progress</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Plan estimates diverge after a load</td><td>Were relevant statistics collected after the change?</td><td>Targeted analyse; compare estimates and actual work again</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Transaction-log allocation grows</td><td>Archive failure, retained replica history, generation rate or another documented cause?</td><td>Repair the responsible consumer; verify progress and storage time remaining</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Files remain large after deletion</td><td>Reusable interior space or an expensive sparse layout?</td><td>Reuse it, or justify a measured rebuild; verify useful space or work saved</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>

<h3 id="clean-up-versions-that-are-no-longer-needed">Clean up versions that are no longer needed</h3>
<p>A long report may need pending orders as they appeared before fulfilment changed them to paid. More vacuum work cannot remove versions that this report is still entitled to read. Figure 1 holds that snapshot while the update commits. Advance to vacuum, then select <em>Report finishes before cleanup</em> at the same step.</p>
<MaintenanceFigure scene="upkeep" number={1}/>
<p>The native run disabled autovacuum on its disposable table so this sequence stayed observable. A repeatable-read transaction read 4,000 orders; another session updated them all. Vacuum reported 4,000 dead versions not yet removable. After the report ended, a second vacuum removed 4,000. Both current-state queries still returned 4,000 paid orders. The workload was correct throughout; the retained earlier view changed cleanup’s result.</p>
<Code code={inspect} lang="sql" themes={themes}/>
<p>The <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/monitoring-stats.html">statistics views</a> give estimates and activity observations, not an exact count of bytes a command will recover. Inspect a fresh observation outside a long monitoring transaction. Match a progress view to its phase; scanned heap blocks are not a percentage of all remaining index and cleanup work. A small vacuum may finish before you can sample it.</p>
<p>Identify the owner before ending a transaction. Also inspect prepared transactions, slot <code>xmin</code> and <code>catalog_xmin</code>, and standby feedback when relevant: history can be required by more than a local report. Agree whether to finish, cancel or reschedule that work, then recheck cleanup. Only after removing impediments should you decide whether the table’s autovacuum thresholds, workers or cost allowance need adjustment under the <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-autovacuum.html">version’s autovacuum controls</a>.</p>
<p>Freezing is an availability duty, including for tables that rarely change. Watch transaction and multixact ages and engine warnings against the configured thresholds. PostgreSQL can refuse new transaction IDs as a wraparound safeguard. Follow its <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/routine-vacuuming.html#VACUUM-FOR-WRAPAROUND">documented recovery procedure</a> if that boundary is reached; repeatedly cancelling protective vacuum postpones the work while consuming the remaining margin. This drill did not manufacture transaction-ID exhaustion. <a href="/review/relational-next/physical-storage/">Physical storage</a> owns where versions and reusable space live.</p>

<h3 id="keep-planner-statistics-useful">Keep planner statistics useful</h3>
<p>Suppose a bulk import turns a mostly pending table into a mostly paid one. The planner needs the new row counts and value distribution. Check the last automatic or manual analyse against that change; an old timestamp or a successful collection before the import answers the wrong question. Run a targeted <code>ANALYZE orders;</code> when justified, with its scan cost in the budget. It samples data to form estimates; fresh does not mean exact.</p>
<p>The native fixture’s analyse completed and its catalogue estimate became 4,000 rows. That verifies collection in this case, without establishing a faster production query. Compare the affected query’s estimates and measured work afterwards. If they still disagree, or a different bottleneck dominates, use <a href="/review/relational-next/query-plans/">query-plan diagnosis</a>. The <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/sql-analyze.html">ANALYZE contract</a> also matters for partitioned tables: do not assume each child’s automatic upkeep refreshes the parent’s combined statistics.</p>

<h3 id="account-for-retained-logs">Account for retained logs</h3>
<p>Transaction logs are retained for recovery and consumers. A failed archive can retain WAL locally even though the application keeps committing. A slot can hold history for a disconnected replica. The <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/view-pg-replication-slots.html">slot catalogue</a> distinguishes the earliest required position from whether that history remains available.</p>
<Code code={logs} lang="sql" themes={themes}/>
<p>Compare successive observations and inspect errors, consumer progress, generated bytes and free-space time. An old failure counter can coexist with current success; a fresh success can coexist with an untested recovery chain. Repair archival through <a href="/review/relational-next/backup-and-restore/">the recovery owner</a> and disconnected replicas through <a href="/review/relational-next/replicas-and-partitions/">the topology owner</a>. Dropping a still-needed slot or expiring history can require reinitialising a member or destroy a recovery target. Never remove active engine log files by hand. <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/continuous-archiving.html">Continuous archiving</a> defines the WAL chain and the consequences of stalled archival.</p>

<h3 id="decide-whether-storage-or-indexes-need-intervention">Decide whether storage or indexes need intervention</h3>
<p>After the native cleanup, the heap remained 1,990,656 bytes. We explicitly disabled end-of-file truncation for that test. The recovered interior space was available for later rows, not returned to the filesystem. That can be exactly the intended outcome for a table that will grow again.</p>
<p>A PostgreSQL <code>VACUUM FULL</code> instead rewrites a table with extra temporary allocation and an <code>ACCESS EXCLUSIVE</code> lock, blocking ordinary reads and writes. Even ordinary vacuum can briefly need that lock for end truncation; <code>TRUNCATE false</code> avoids that phase. The <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/sql-vacuum.html">command contract</a> defines those distinct costs. In our run a reader held a table lock, and a separate <code>VACUUM FULL</code> with a 200 ms lock timeout failed before rewriting. The timeout was the expected no-go result.</p>
<p>For an index, establish that sparse storage or a required repair has a useful outcome before rebuilding. PostgreSQL’s <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/sql-reindex.html"><code>REINDEX CONCURRENTLY</code></a> allows normal writes during most work but performs extra scans and waits, consumes space and load, and can leave invalid remnants after failure. It is not a promise of zero blocking. Our tiny concurrent primary-key rebuild completed and left a valid index; it demonstrated the command, not a need to rebuild every index periodically. <a href="/review/relational-next/indexes/">Choosing indexes</a> owns the workload benefit; <a href="/review/relational-next/schema-changes/">schema changes</a> owns index rollout and lock mechanics.</p>

<h2 id="fit-maintenance-within-the-service-budget">Fit maintenance within the service budget</h2>
<p>Turn the diagnosed problem into one change record. The shop’s first action is to let the report finish and repeat ordinary cleanup. A rewrite would spend interruption and extra storage before proving that either is necessary. If representative evidence later justifies shrinking a sparse table, budget its peak allocation before the space saving.</p>
<p>Figure 2 starts with a measured target expressed as invented planning sizes: a 20 GB heap is expected to become 8 GB. Advance through the replacement and retained-log allocations, then reduce the available change allowance.</p>
<MaintenanceFigure scene="budget" number={2}/>
<p>Reserve space for the new heap, rebuilt indexes, work files, WAL and continuing growth while the old allocation remains. Include replica and archive capacity: log generated quickly may be retained for much longer. Derive the estimates and duration from a representative copy with comparable limits. The figure’s 22 GB peak is arithmetic under its assumptions, not a universal multiplier.</p>
<p>For that proposed rewrite, the operator records a five-minute maintenance window, a two-second lock-acquisition allowance, the measured extra-space estimate with contingency, and baseline order latency and error limits. These are illustrative allowances to accept or reject in rehearsal. Pause reports, coordinate backups and avoid overlapping rebuilds. Watch host storage and I/O, application errors and latency, lock waiters and the <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/progress-reporting.html">operation’s progress view</a>. <code>VACUUM FULL</code> reports through <code>pg_stat_progress_cluster</code>; an index rebuild uses <code>pg_stat_progress_create_index</code>.</p>
<p>Assign someone to stop on a breached gate. Cancellation is not instant recovery of all resources or work: wait for the backend and lock release, remeasure space, inspect any invalid concurrent-index remnants and verify normal traffic. Ordinary vacuum progress is not simply undone as though it were one application transaction. If the measured operation cannot fit safely alongside requests, reduce competing work or use an agreed interruption window. <a href="/review/relational-next/monitoring-and-capacity/">Monitoring and capacity</a> supplies the continuing headroom and intervention budget.</p>

<h2 id="establish-an-upgrade-path-before-choosing-a-date">Establish an upgrade path before choosing a date</h2>
<p>Maintenance changes the state of the existing service; an upgrade changes the software interpreting that state. A PostgreSQL minor update stays within a major version’s storage format and normally replaces binaries while the server is stopped. Read the exact release notes anyway for corrective actions. A major change needs a supported data transition and application checks. PostgreSQL’s <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/upgrading.html">upgrade guidance</a> distinguishes these paths and calls out intervening release notes when skipping majors.</p>
<table><caption>Choose the method against the service requirement</caption><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Resources and interruption</th><th>Return-path question</th></tr></thead><tbody>
 <tr><td>Logical export and load</td><td>New environment; export, load and object-build time</td><td>Does the old service remain intact, and what writes occurred after the export?</td></tr>
 <tr><td><code>pg_upgrade --copy</code></td><td>Stopped source and destination for the actual upgrade; independent copied files</td><td>Can the old snapshot meet the required state after new writes?</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Documented link or clone transfer</td><td>Different file-sharing and filesystem requirements; rehearse the chosen mode</td><td>When does starting the new server invalidate the old files?</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Replacement with logical catch-up</td><td>Two running environments and change capture; controlled cutover</td><td>How are final history, missing object types and later writes handled?</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>For the shop’s bounded rehearsal, choose copy mode: it costs additional storage but preserves independently restartable old files. Figure 3 compares the link-mode boundary. Advance to <em>Start the new cluster</em>, then change the transfer mode.</p>
<MaintenanceFigure scene="upgrade" number={3}/>
<p>In link mode, starting the upgraded server writes files shared with the old cluster, making the old cluster unsafe to start. Clone mode has a different contract and filesystem prerequisites. Follow the chosen mode’s <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/pgupgrade.html">pg_upgrade procedure</a>; never try old binaries on the upgraded directory. Restoring an independent supported copy is a separate operation, owned by <a href="/review/relational-next/backup-and-restore/">backup and restoration</a>.</p>
<p>Inventory the exact source and target, extension versions and target libraries, SQL behaviour, drivers and pools, authentication and configuration, collations, custom dictionaries, scheduled jobs, observability and backup tools. Test the actual application against the target. A collation-library change can invalidate an index’s ordering assumptions; clearing a version warning alone does not rebuild affected objects. Use the <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/sql-altercollation.html">collation refresh procedure</a> and the <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/release-18.html">target’s migration notes</a> for required work.</p>
<p>Do not plan a mixed-major physical standby as a generic rolling PostgreSQL upgrade. Establish the selected replication path’s compatibility and member replacement sequence with <a href="/review/relational-next/replicas-and-partitions/">the topology owner</a>. Logical catch-up can support different majors under its own restrictions; <a href="/review/relational-next/database-moves/">database moves</a> owns that cutover procedure.</p>
<p>A hosted service changes who performs the steps. For RDS PostgreSQL, query the actual source’s <a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/USER_UpgradeDBInstance.PostgreSQL.MajorVersion.html">valid upgrade targets</a>, then check the chosen deployment’s parameter groups, extensions, precheck logs, replicas and interruption behaviour. The <a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/USER_UpgradeDBInstance.PostgreSQL.MajorVersion.Process.html">major-upgrade process</a> defines preparation; the <a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/USER_UpgradeDBInstance.PostgreSQL.html">engine-upgrade contract</a> distinguishes instance and cluster transitions. Record automatic maintenance settings, who can schedule or initiate changes and what the provider controls. The local drill is no evidence that a hosted upgrade or fallback works.</p>

<h2 id="rehearse-the-upgrade-and-the-way-back">Rehearse the upgrade and the way back</h2>
<p>Use an isolated representative copy with the actual extensions, configuration and application checks, excluding live workers and external effects. Establish usable independent recovery inputs first. Measure shutdown-to-usable-service time, command stages and peak storage separately. Include validation, statistics work, reconnection and degraded replica capacity in the service interval.</p>
<p><a href={rehearsalUrl} download="rehearse.py">Download the disposable maintenance and upgrade rehearsal</a>. It needs Python 3, PostgreSQL 17.6 and 18.6 server tools, and an ordinary OS account allowed to initialise local servers. Supply your installed binary directories and a new evidence filename:</p>
<Code code={run} lang="sh" themes={themes}/>
<p>The harness creates fresh private temporary clusters and unused loopback ports, using local trust authentication only inside this drill. It contacts no existing database, refuses to overwrite evidence, retains commands and server logs, then stops its servers and removes its temporary data. The maintenance fixture contains 4,000 orders; the upgrade fixture contains only orders 101 and 102, totalling 4,000 minor currency units. Its small scale tests transitions, not operating capacity.</p>
<p>The first upgrade check deliberately meets an unsupported <code>regproc</code> user-table column. It rejects the attempt. Restarting the old cluster still produces its two orders. The harness removes this throwaway incompatibility, stops the source and repeats the check successfully. In a real schema, investigate the object and preserve its meaning through a supported repair; dropping a business dependency to clear a check is not an upgrade plan.</p>
<Code code={upgrade} lang="sh" themes={themes}/>
<p>The real 17.6 → 18.6 copy upgrade then completed. New 18.6 showed the same orders and total. A deliberately wrong acceptance condition, “there must be three orders”, failed before traffic. Stopping the new cluster and restarting the intact old one returned the correct two-order fixture. This rehearsed a failed validation gate without claiming that the upgrade had corrupted data.</p>
<p>Finally the new cluster accepted order 103 for 500. New total: 4,500. Old total: still 4,000. A safe old data directory is now a stale service state. Switching the connection back would hide a confirmed order. A planned return after writes needs supported reverse movement or forward recovery and reconciliation, with <a href="/review/relational-next/database-moves/">the live-change owner</a>. An independent restore alone also returns only the history it contains.</p>
<p>The command took 1.823 seconds in the first native run. The old stopped directory held about 39.9 MB of logical file lengths, and the final new directory about 41.5 MB. These are tiny local observations; file lengths are not peak physical disk allocation. No representative application traffic, extension upgrade, link-mode upgrade, crash during transfer, replica rebuild or hosted service was tested. Measure those parts of the chosen production path before accepting its budget.</p>

<h2 id="execute-with-explicit-gates">Execute with explicit gates</h2>
<p>Write the gates into the runbook before closing traffic. One operator executes; a named change lead owns the proceed or return decision. The application owner accepts the facts and workflows. For the worked copy-mode path:</p>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Copy-mode upgrade runbook and stop gates"><table><thead><tr><th>Step and owner</th><th>Evidence required to continue</th><th>When to stop</th></tr></thead><tbody>
 <tr><td>Prepare — operator and recovery owner</td><td>Pinned tools, compatible destination, passing rehearsal and preflight, required libraries, measured budgets and verified recovery access</td><td>Unsupported dependency, missing recovery evidence or insufficient allowance</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Close admission — application owner</td><td>Requests and dependent jobs paused; in-flight outcomes resolved; old writer excluded from new work; final known data boundary recorded</td><td>Unknown writer or unresolved work could change the source</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Stop and upgrade — operator</td><td>Both clusters stopped; intended paths and transfer mode checked; target pg_upgrade run completes with retained logs</td><td>Error or budget breach: preserve evidence and apply the rehearsed mode-specific return</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Start in isolation — operator</td><td>Target engine and configuration; emitted rebuild, extension and statistics work completed as required</td><td>Object or configuration check fails; keep traffic excluded</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Accept and admit — application owner and lead</td><td>Known facts, privileges and controlled application write/read pass; errors, latency and headroom fit the baseline</td><td>Any required result unproved; record whether new writes already exist before choosing fallback</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Protect and retire — recovery and topology owners</td><td>New-state backups and restoration, archive continuity and compatible replicas accepted; later work accounted for</td><td>Keep the previous option while new-state recovery remains unproved</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>If a check fails during execution, the mode and reached stage decide which procedure is supported. A failed preflight is different from a failed schema transfer or a new server already writing shared files. Inspect the target tool’s logs and generated instructions. Reinitialise a failed destination for a fresh attempt where required; do not repeatedly rerun an upgrade against an uncertain partially changed state. A provider-run transition replaces the executable steps with its supported API, event and recovery gates while leaving application acceptance yours.</p>

<h2 id="prove-completion-and-update-the-next-run">Prove completion and update the next run</h2>
<p>The engine version is necessary evidence, but one successful <code>SELECT 1</code> cannot prove that Mina can retrieve an order. Compare known identifiers, values and relationships, object and extension state, indexes and constraints, permissions and generated identifiers. Test a controlled write and its required read through new application connections. The native fixture is deliberately simpler than the shop’s real schema.</p>
<Code code={verify} lang="sql" themes={themes}/>
<p>PostgreSQL 18 can transfer most optimiser statistics, but not every statistics category. Follow the exact tool’s emitted post-upgrade instructions rather than copying an older runbook. The two <code>vacuumdb</code> steps shown above ran successfully in this rehearsal. Resume enough representative traffic to compare error rate, latency, plans and resource headroom with the pre-change baseline; a cold cache and new plans can change usable performance after correctness checks pass.</p>
<p>Figure 4 starts at command completion. Compare rejection before traffic with acceptance followed by a new order. Watch the old copy’s row set when you reach the proposed return.</p>
<MaintenanceFigure scene="accept" number={4}/>
<p>Verify upkeep progress, replication and archival through their owning procedures. Establish recovery coverage for the new engine, configuration and extension state, including a usable restore, before retiring the older option. A pre-upgrade physical backup belongs with the old engine’s recovery environment; it is not automatically a new-major restore input. Preserve the older binaries and dependencies that the retained recovery plan requires.</p>
<p>Record actual interruption through accepted application service, peak allocation, waits, command output, validation results, accepted or unresolved later writes, deviations and follow-up owners. If the expected space saving did not occur, or latency exceeds the allowance, the change is not complete merely because the process exited successfully. Update the next run from those observations and repeat the relevant rehearsal after material changes.</p>

